@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:build;security] native-windows-dotnet-release
The native Windows Mila release MUST be built from `Mila.Windows.csproj` with the
pinned .NET/Windows App SDK toolchain and the staged `mila_native.dll`. Its
published output MUST NOT contain retired client engine/runtime files, retired runtime snapshots,
`retired-runtime metadata` metadata, or retired client-generated Windows runner assets. The release
MUST remain runnable without a retired client SDK or `retired-runtime` executable on PATH.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:verification] native-windows-dotnet-release-audit
`verify-release.ps1` MUST inspect a publish directory, require the managed
executable and native library, and reject any retired client runtime artifacts. This is a
packaging boundary check; native Windows execution and signing remain covered by
Windows CI evidence.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;reliability] native-windows-lifecycle-adapters
Windows lifecycle adapters MUST keep authority in the Rust session: the
deployment picker fences approval and session work by selection generation
(`Interop/DeploymentPicker.cs`), sign-in polls `whoami` on the same Rust
session and binds approval links to the selected host and HTTPS port
(`Interop/SignInCoordinator.cs`), terminal attach names a server session from
`terminal new` and reconnect rejoins it, distinguishing idle, closed and lost
PTYs (`Interop/TerminalStream.cs`), WNS exchange sends the
channel only to Susan and the push key to Porsche only through Rust stdin
(`Interop/WindowsPushRegistration.cs`), and share completion is navigation
only (`Interop/ExternalActivation.cs`). The in-source `mila-windows-*` notes
state each obligation; `windows-dotnet-smoke` exercises them hermetically.
